WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.187
This example shows two versions of the same pivot cell.

00:00:03.187 --> 00:00:05.497
The first CASE has no ELSE zero, so a missing Q3 response stays NULL.

00:00:05.497 --> 00:00:08.362
The second explicitly forces zero.

00:00:08.362 --> 00:00:10.907
Run the query and find respondents with no Q3 observation.

00:00:10.907 --> 00:00:15.882
Ask whether zero is a real score or simply a convenient placeholder.

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:17.782
That distinction affects later averages and models.

00:00:17.782 --> 00:00:20.262
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:20.262 --> 00:00:26.082
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:26.082 --> 00:00:28.372
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:28.372 --> 00:00:32.367
In a real setting such as a survey project reshaping question responses for analysis,

00:00:32.367 --> 00:00:36.667
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:38.082
before discussing syntax.

00:00:38.082 --> 00:00:43.600
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
